===persian===

از پشتِ پُل،
قدیمی‌ترین پُل
چندین هزار بار تو سرکشیده‌ای...
و... چندهزار عاشق
با دیدنِ تو... آه کشیدند؟...
و... چندهزار دیده
خط‌های نور را...
از اشک تا آبِ رود، تکرار کرده‌اند؟...
زاینده‌رود را...
ای عابرِ قدیمی
ای شاهدِ جوانی پل، فانوسِ عاشقان،
ای ماه...
ماهِ بزرگ...
ماهِ تمام...
از پشتِ پل درود...
با تک درخت پیر،
از پای پل... سلام.
اصفهان ۱۳۶۸/۱۱/۲۱

===machine===

From behind the bridge,
the oldest bridge —
how many thousand times have you risen over it...
and... how many thousand lovers
sighed at the sight of you?...
and... how many thousand eyes
repeated the lines of light,
from tear to river water?...
The Zayandeh-rud...
O ancient wayfarer,
O witness of the bridge's youth, lantern of lovers,
O moon...
great moon...
full moon...
a greeting from behind the bridge...
with the one old tree,
from the foot of the bridge... salaam.
Isfahan 1368/11/21

===lantern===

From behind the bridge,
the oldest bridge —
how many thousand times have you risen over it...
and... how many thousand lovers
sighed at the sight of you?...
and... how many thousand eyes
have repeated the lines of light
from tear to river-water?...
The Zayandeh-rud...
O ancient wayfarer,
O witness of the bridge's youth, lantern of lovers,
O moon...
great moon...
full moon...
a greeting from behind the bridge —
and with the one old tree,
from the foot of the bridge... salaam.

Isfahan 1368/11/21

===translation===

===footnotes===

Isfahan has a series of old bridges over the Zayandeh-rud; the oldest of them, the
Shahrestan, is Sasanian in origin, and the poet does not say which he is standing behind. The moon
is addressed as عابرِ قدیمی, an ancient passer-by, and as the witness of the bridge's *youth* —
which is the poem's one sharp move: the bridge is the old thing here, and the moon watched it be
young.

The greeting comes twice and changes language: درود, the old Persian salutation, then سلام. The
English keeps the second untranslated so that the poem ends the warmer, more ordinary way it does
in Persian.

